                            'objective' => 'Use commas and punctuation correctly, and choose the right relative pronoun.',
                            'xp' => 35, 'minutes' => 18,
                            'sections' => [
                                ['type' => 'intro', 'title' => 'Two types of information', 'body' => "Compare these:\n\n**Defining:** The employee **who submitted the proposal** received praise.\n(Which employee? The one who submitted the proposal—this identifies them.)\n\n**Non-defining:** Sarah, **who submitted the proposal**, received praise.\n(We already know who Sarah is. The extra clause just adds detail.)\n\nOne uses commas; the other doesn't. The difference is crucial for clarity and correctness."],
                                ['type' => 'explanation', 'title' => 'Rules for relative clauses', 'body' => "**Defining (restrictive) relative clause:**\n- **No commas** around the clause.\n- The clause identifies or specifies which person/thing you mean.\n- Can use that, which, who, whom, whose, or omit the pronoun.\n  - The company **that hired me** is international.\n  - The contract **which we signed** is binding.\n  - The person **you met** works here. (pronoun omitted)\n\n**Non-defining (non-restrictive) relative clause:**\n- **Commas around the clause** (or dash, parentheses).\n- The clause adds extra, non-essential information.\n- Use who, which, whom, whose, or where/when—**never that**, and rarely omit the pronoun.\n  - The CEO, **who is 65**, will retire next year.\n  - The conference, **which lasted three days**, was productive.\n\n**Punctuation is not optional—it changes meaning!**\n- \"My brother **who is a doctor** lives in Jakarta.\" (I have multiple brothers; this is the doctor one.)\n- \"My brother, **who is a doctor**, lives in Jakarta.\" (I have one brother, and he happens to be a doctor.)", 'body_id' => 'Defining clause: tidak ada koma, identifikasi orang/benda. Non-defining clause: ada koma, informasi tambahan.'],
